rding processes. Employees are regularly informed about the consequences of non-compliance with security policies, and the model is highlighted in interactive workshops, emphasizing the roles of both employees and customers in maintaining security. The engineering team at Tanager Geospatial collaborates with security personnel to ensure security under this model. It also informs customers of their obligations regarding personnel vetting and is aligned with the visitor management protocol at Tanager Geospatial. Tanager Geospatial specifically ensures infrastructure standards, while customers manage their own data integrity. This model also applies to the secure destruction of media, where both Tanager Geospatial and its customers have roles. The policy review process at Tanager Geospatial also aligns with this model.\n\nSimilarly, Tanager Atlas implements the Shared-Responsibility Model across its operations, particularly in areas like privileged access management, where it emphasizes role-based access control (RBAC). It involves collaboration with customers on monitoring, user provisioning, and reviewing access control changes. For data security, Tanager Atlas manages HSMs and secure key storage, while customers are responsible for key access and usage policies, managing their encryption keys, and actively identifying and classifying their data. The model also guides their approach to secure key generation, key destruction policies, and integrating security testing. Tanager Atlas applies this model to integrate Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST) findings into development processes, monitor vulnerabilities in third-party libraries, and ensure compliance with data standards by third-party vendors. It is also a rigorous framework for applying data minimization principles, documenting and tracking sensitive data deletion, and handling subject rights requests. The model drives the process of reviewing hardening practices, prioritizes log sources based on potenti