{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How do you assess the effectiveness of your staff's understanding of encryption and key management protocols?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs a rigorous compliance program to evaluate our staff's grasp of encryption and key management protocols. We conduct quarterly assessments aligned with ISO 27001 standards, ensuring our team is well-versed in current practices. Our internal audits focus on key management processes and encryption methodologies, reinforcing the importance of these protocols in our IaaS offerings. Staff training is mandatory, with completion rates tracked for compliance."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: Can you outline the process for generating encryption keys, including any specific algorithms or standards followed?\nA: Kestrel Cloud follows strict compliance protocols in key generation, utilizing AES-256 encryption. Our key management process is governed by our Kestrel Compliance Framework, which mandates key rotation every 6 months. Customers must manage their own key lifecycle, while we ensure the underlying infrastructure meets industry standards. All encryption keys are generated in a secure environment to mitigate risk."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: Can you describe the mechanisms in place to protect encryption keys used for data at rest, including any access controls?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs strict access controls for encryption keys, governed by our compliance program, Kestrel Secure. Keys are managed using a centralized key management system that complies with ISO 27001 standards. Access is limited to designated security roles, and all key-related activities are logged for audit purposes. Customers retain responsibility for key rotation and management within their environments, while we ensure the infrastructure remains secure."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What auditing practices are in place to monitor the use of encryption keys, and how frequently are these audits conducted?\nA: Kestrel Cloud conducts bi-annual audits of encryption key usage as part of our compliance program. These audits are guided by our internal standards, ensuring that key management practices meet regulatory requirements. Customers retain responsibility for their own key management, while we provide the necessary infrastructure to support secure operations. Our control references are documented in our compliance framework, which is available to clients upon request."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What processes are in place to manage sensitive information such as API keys and secrets throughout the application lifecycle?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs a rigorous compliance program that mandates the use of encryption for all API keys and secrets. Access is strictly controlled through role-based access management, and we conduct quarterly reviews to ensure adherence to our internal standards. Customers are responsible for managing their own secrets within their environments, while we provide the necessary tools and documentation to facilitate secure practices."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What steps do you take to ensure that your encryption practices remain effective against evolving cybersecurity threats?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs a rigorous compliance program, including regular reviews of our encryption protocols aligned with NIST standards. We conduct quarterly assessments to ensure our encryption methods, including TLS 1.3 for data in transit, remain effective. Customers are responsible for managing their own encryption keys, while we focus on maintaining a secure infrastructure. Our internal audits ensure that we adapt swiftly to any new threats identified in the cybersecurity landscape."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How do you ensure that key generation processes are secure and resistant to unauthorized access or compromise?\nA: Kestrel Cloud adheres to strict compliance standards such as ISO 27001 and SOC 2. Key generation processes are managed through our proprietary tool, Kestrel KeyGen, which ensures that keys are generated in a secure environment with limited access. Our compliance program mandates regular assessments and audits to maintain the integrity of these processes. Customers are responsible for managing their own encryption keys, while we ensure the underlying infrastructure remains secure."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What documentation is maintained regarding your cryptographic policies and procedures, and how is this documentation kept up to date?\nA: Kestrel Cloud maintains a robust set of documentation for our cryptographic policies, adhering to the ISO 27001 standard. This documentation is reviewed annually as part of our compliance program, Kestrel Secure, to ensure it meets evolving regulatory requirements. We emphasize that customers are responsible for managing their own encryption keys, while we provide the necessary tools and infrastructure for secure data handling. Our policies are accessible through our compliance portal for transparency."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: Can you explain the encryption standards used for disk encryption on corporate devices, including the key management practices that support these standards?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs AES-256 encryption for disk storage, adhering to our compliance program, Kestrel Secure. Key management is primarily the customer's responsibility, although we provide tools for secure key generation and storage. Compliance with NIST standards is maintained, and we conduct quarterly reviews of our encryption practices to ensure alignment with industry regulations. Our customers can leverage our APIs to integrate their key management solutions seamlessly."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: Can you describe any third-party audits or assessments that have been conducted on your cryptographic practices, and what were the outcomes?\nA: Kestrel Cloud conducts biannual assessments of our cryptographic controls as part of our compliance program, Kestrel Secure. These evaluations are aligned with ISO 27001 and SOC 2 Type II standards. Recent audits verified our strong encryption protocols, but highlighted areas for improvement in customer-managed encryption keys. Our clients are responsible for key management, ensuring they understand their role in our shared responsibility model."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What processes are in place to update your encryption practices in response to emerging threats or vulnerabilities?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs a strict compliance program that mandates quarterly reviews of our encryption practices. We reference industry standards such as NIST and ISO 27001 to guide our updates. Any identified vulnerabilities trigger an immediate assessment, followed by necessary changes. Our customers are responsible for their encryption keys, while we ensure our infrastructure remains secure and compliant."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How do you evaluate the security of the cryptographic algorithms you use, and what criteria do you apply for their selection?\nA: Kestrel Cloud evaluates cryptographic algorithms based on compliance with our internal standards and industry benchmarks. We adhere to the ISO 27001 framework and regularly assess algorithm effectiveness through our compliance program. The selection criteria focus on algorithms that have undergone extensive peer review and are widely accepted in the industry. Customers are responsible for implementing their own key management processes, while we provide guidance on best practices."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: Who is responsible for approving changes to the encryption protocols used for data in transit within your organization?\nA: Changes to encryption protocols at Kestrel Cloud are approved by the Compliance Officer as part of our internal governance framework. Our compliance program, Kestrel Secure, mandates a review every six months, ensuring that we adhere to industry standards such as PCI DSS and GDPR. Customers are responsible for the encryption of data at rest, while we manage data in transit according to our established protocols."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How often are your encryption keys rotated, and what process do you follow to ensure that this is done securely?\nA: Kestrel Cloud rotates encryption keys every 60 days in accordance with our compliance program, ensuring adherence to ISO 27001 standards. Our automated key management system handles the rotation process, while customers are required to manage their own key access policies. We maintain strict logging and monitoring to ensure compliance and security throughout the key lifecycle."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What is your policy for the destruction of encryption keys, and how do you ensure that this process is followed consistently?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs a rigorous key destruction policy in accordance with our compliance program, Kestrel Secure. Keys are destroyed after a defined retention period of 90 days post-decommissioning. This process is enforced through strict access controls and regular audits to ensure compliance with industry standards. Customers must manage their own encryption keys, while we provide the framework for secure key lifecycle management."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What encryption standards are utilized for data at rest within your cloud environment, and how do you ensure compliance with these standards?\nA: Kestrel Cloud utilizes AES-256 encryption for data at rest, aligning with our Kestrel Compliance Framework. Compliance is maintained through regular assessments against ISO 27001 standards. Customers are responsible for managing their encryption keys, while we ensure the infrastructure is secure and compliant. Our control references guide customers in maintaining their own compliance."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How do you manage and document key access requests, and who has the authority to approve such requests?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs a strict access control framework governed by our Compliance Program. Key access requests are logged and reviewed monthly, with approvals granted solely by designated compliance officers. This process adheres to ISO 27001 standards, ensuring that only authorized personnel can access sensitive information. Customers are responsible for managing their own user permissions within the Kestrel Cloud platform, maintaining a clear delineation of responsibilities."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: Do you utilize a key escrow system, and if so, what criteria determine the conditions under which keys are escrowed?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs a key escrow system, governed by our stringent compliance program, Kestrel Secure. Keys are escrowed only under defined conditions, such as regulatory requirements or specific customer agreements. Our control framework dictates these criteria, ensuring that key management aligns with industry standards and best practices. Customers retain responsibility for key lifecycle management, while we maintain oversight of the escrow process."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What role does hardware security modules (HSMs) play in your key management strategy, and how are they integrated into your overall architecture?\nA: Kestrel Cloud utilizes HSMs as a critical component of our key management framework. We adhere to strict compliance standards, including ISO 27001 and PCI DSS, which guide our implementation. HSMs are deployed to manage encryption keys, while customers maintain control over their own key lifecycle management. This delineation of responsibilities is essential to our compliance program, ensuring that both parties understand their roles in securing sensitive data."}
