{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What methods do you use to monitor and control outbound traffic to ensure compliance with security policies and prevent data leakage?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs strict outbound traffic controls as part of our compliance program, Kestrel Secure. We utilize automated monitoring tools that assess traffic against predefined security policies. All outbound traffic is logged and reviewed weekly to ensure adherence to compliance standards. The responsibility for data classification and policy enforcement lies primarily with the customer, while we facilitate the necessary controls to support their efforts."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How do you ensure that all network segmentation practices are documented and communicated to relevant stakeholders?\nA: Kestrel Cloud ensures network segmentation practices are documented in accordance with our compliance program. This information is communicated to stakeholders through quarterly reviews and our policy management system. We reference industry standards such as ISO 27001 and NIST SP 800-53, ensuring that our controls are aligned with best practices. Stakeholders are responsible for understanding their obligations under our shared responsibility model, which delineates clear roles."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How are security policies related to network segmentation enforced across different departments within your organization?\nA: Kestrel Cloud enforces network segmentation policies through our compliance program, which adheres to ISO 27001 standards. Each department is required to follow strict access controls and segmentation protocols, with quarterly reviews to ensure compliance. Our internal security team conducts regular assessments, and we provide clients with guidelines to maintain their own responsibilities in this shared model."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: Can you describe the process for segmenting your internal network to limit lateral movement in the event of a breach?\nA: Kestrel Cloud implements stringent network segmentation practices as part of our compliance program, Kestrel Secure. We utilize virtual private clouds (VPCs) to isolate workloads and enforce access controls based on the principle of least privilege. Regular reviews are conducted to ensure adherence to industry standards, such as ISO 27001, and our customers are responsible for managing their own security groups and firewall rules within their allocated VPCs. This ensures that any lateral movement is effectively contained within the customer's environment."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How do you ensure that egress controls are effectively implemented to prevent unauthorized data exfiltration from your network?\nA: Kestrel Cloud enforces egress controls through our compliance program, which adheres to ISO 27001 standards. We utilize automated tools to monitor and log all outbound traffic, ensuring that any unauthorized attempts are flagged immediately. Our customers are responsible for configuring their own security groups, but we provide guidelines to streamline this process. Regular internal audits verify the effectiveness of our controls, maintaining a secure environment for all users of Kestrel Cloud."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What mechanisms are in place to monitor and log traffic at the perimeter of your network to detect unauthorized access attempts?\nA: Kestrel Cloud integrates perimeter security measures with our compliance program, Kestrel Secure. We utilize automated logging mechanisms that capture all incoming and outgoing traffic for analysis. Our monitoring tools, aligned with ISO 27001 standards, provide real-time alerts for unauthorized access attempts. Customers are required to maintain their own security configurations while we oversee the infrastructure's integrity. Logs are reviewed bi-weekly to ensure compliance and security."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: Can you describe the incident response procedures for handling detected anomalies in egress traffic?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs a rigorous incident response framework that aligns with our compliance program, Kestrel Secure. Upon detection of anomalies in egress traffic, our team initiates a response within 30 minutes, leveraging automated alerting systems. The responsibility for monitoring and initial anomaly detection lies with the customer, while Kestrel Cloud manages the remediation processes and compliance reporting. Our adherence to industry standards ensures that all incidents are documented and reviewed systematically."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What role do automated tools play in managing and enforcing egress controls across your network?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs automated tools to enforce egress controls as part of our compliance program, Kestrel Guard. These tools continuously monitor data egress points and ensure adherence to our established security standards. We maintain strict control over egress traffic, and while our tools provide significant oversight, customers must actively participate in defining their specific egress policies to align with their compliance requirements."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What logging practices are in place for egress traffic, and how long is this data retained for forensic analysis?\nA: Kestrel Cloud maintains strict logging practices for egress traffic as part of our compliance program. We log relevant egress traffic data for a duration of 30 days, following the standards set forth in our Kestrel Compliance Framework. This data includes essential metadata necessary for audit trails. Customers are responsible for their own logging configurations, and we provide APIs to facilitate this process as needed."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What specific actions do you take if a subprocessor is found to be non-compliant with your security requirements during ongoing monitoring?\nA: Kestrel Cloud adheres to strict compliance protocols. If a subprocessor is found non-compliant, we will invoke our compliance program, specifically the Kestrel Compliance Framework. Immediate actions include a compliance review and potential suspension of the subprocessor's access to our services until the issue is resolved. We reference ISO 27001 standards throughout this process to ensure robust risk management."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How frequently are your egress filtering rules reviewed and updated to adapt to new threats or changes in your network architecture?\nA: Kestrel Cloud updates its egress filtering rules biannually, in line with our compliance program, Kestrel Secure. This process is governed by our internal security standards, which are aligned with industry best practices. Customers are responsible for their own configurations, while we ensure our core infrastructure is fortified against evolving threats. Regular audits support our commitment to maintaining a secure environment."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How does your organization handle exceptions to standard access control policies, and what approval process is followed?\nA: Kestrel Cloud follows a strict compliance program for handling access control exceptions. Any deviation from standard policies requires documented approval from a designated Compliance Officer. This process is in line with our ISO 27001 framework. Requests are logged and tracked for audit purposes, ensuring accountability and traceability. We emphasize the importance of adhering to these controls to maintain the security posture of Kestrel Cloud."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What specific technologies do you utilize to protect against DDoS attacks, and how are they integrated into your overall security architecture?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs a combination of network traffic analysis and automated mitigation tools to counter DDoS attacks. Our compliance program, Kestrel Secure, mandates regular assessments of these controls. Customers are expected to manage their own firewall settings while we handle the backend protections. This delineation of responsibilities is crucial for maintaining a robust security posture across our IaaS and managed-container offerings."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What steps do you take to ensure that your exit strategy for terminating vendor relationships includes secure data handling and transfer protocols?\nA: Kestrel Cloud adheres to strict compliance standards during vendor terminations. Our exit strategy mandates the use of the Kestrel Compliance Framework, which includes secure data handling and transfer protocols. All data must be securely wiped or transferred within 30 days of termination, ensuring that control remains with the client throughout the process. We follow ISO 27001 guidelines to ensure data integrity and confidentiality."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What strategies do you employ to manage and secure wireless networks within your organization?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs a rigorous compliance program that adheres to industry standards such as ISO 27001 and NIST SP 800-53. Our wireless networks are secured using enterprise-grade encryption and monitored continuously for unauthorized access. We conduct biannual audits to ensure compliance with our internal policies. Customers are responsible for managing their own access control policies within Kestrel Cloud, while we maintain the underlying infrastructure security."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How is the effectiveness of your firewall rules assessed, and what criteria are used to determine necessary adjustments?\nA: Kestrel Cloud evaluates firewall effectiveness through biannual compliance checks aligned with our Kestrel Compliance Program. Adjustments are made based on the NIST Cybersecurity Framework and specific threat intelligence reports. The responsibility for maintaining firewall rules is shared with customers, who must ensure their configurations meet our guidelines."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What criteria does your organization use to determine whether an incident requires escalation to senior management or external parties, and how is this communicated?\nA: Kestrel Cloud follows strict criteria outlined in our Compliance Framework. Incidents are escalated to senior management if they meet thresholds defined in our Incident Response Policy. Communication is executed via our internal ticketing system, ensuring prompt documentation and tracking. External parties are notified as per regulatory requirements. The customer is responsible for monitoring their environments to identify potential issues, while we manage the infrastructure layer."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: Can you describe the process for reviewing and addressing findings from external audits, including how corrective actions are tracked and reported?\nA: Kestrel Cloud follows a strict protocol for reviewing external audit findings, adhering to our compliance program, Kestrel Compliance Assurance. Findings are evaluated within two weeks, and corrective actions are assigned to responsible teams. We utilize our Compliance Tracker tool to monitor progress and ensure timely resolution. Reports are generated quarterly to assess the effectiveness of our corrective measures and maintain compliance with industry standards."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What methods do you use to document and track the deletion of sensitive data from your systems?\nA: Kestrel Cloud adheres to strict compliance protocols, including our Kestrel Compliance Program. We utilize automated scripts to track data deletion across our infrastructure, ensuring that sensitive data is securely removed within 30 days of a deletion request. Each deletion is logged in our centralized compliance dashboard, which is regularly reviewed for adherence to internal policies and external regulations. Customers are responsible for initiating the deletion process, while we ensure the secure execution of these requests."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How does your organization handle the authentication of users accessing services from external networks or devices?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs strict authentication protocols for users accessing our IaaS platform. Our compliance program mandates the use of multi-factor authentication for all external access. Users must authenticate via secure tokens issued by our system. The responsibility for maintaining user credentials lies with the customer, while Kestrel Cloud ensures that the authentication infrastructure complies with industry standards such as ISO 27001 and NIST SP 800-53."}
