{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: Can you describe the process for segmenting your internal network to limit lateral movement in the event of a breach?\nA: Kestrel Cloud implements stringent network segmentation practices as part of our compliance program, Kestrel Secure. We utilize virtual private clouds (VPCs) to isolate workloads and enforce access controls based on the principle of least privilege. Regular reviews are conducted to ensure adherence to industry standards, such as ISO 27001, and our customers are responsible for managing their own security groups and firewall rules within their allocated VPCs. This ensures that any lateral movement is effectively contained within the customer's environment."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How are security policies related to network segmentation enforced across different departments within your organization?\nA: Kestrel Cloud enforces network segmentation policies through our compliance program, which adheres to ISO 27001 standards. Each department is required to follow strict access controls and segmentation protocols, with quarterly reviews to ensure compliance. Our internal security team conducts regular assessments, and we provide clients with guidelines to maintain their own responsibilities in this shared model."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What specific technologies do you utilize to protect against DDoS attacks, and how are they integrated into your overall security architecture?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs a combination of network traffic analysis and automated mitigation tools to counter DDoS attacks. Our compliance program, Kestrel Secure, mandates regular assessments of these controls. Customers are expected to manage their own firewall settings while we handle the backend protections. This delineation of responsibilities is crucial for maintaining a robust security posture across our IaaS and managed-container offerings."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How do you ensure that all network segmentation practices are documented and communicated to relevant stakeholders?\nA: Kestrel Cloud ensures network segmentation practices are documented in accordance with our compliance program. This information is communicated to stakeholders through quarterly reviews and our policy management system. We reference industry standards such as ISO 27001 and NIST SP 800-53, ensuring that our controls are aligned with best practices. Stakeholders are responsible for understanding their obligations under our shared responsibility model, which delineates clear roles."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What training programs are in place to ensure that your staff understands the importance of network security and segmentation?\nA: Kestrel Cloud implements a rigorous training program as part of our compliance framework. Staff members complete annual training on network security and segmentation, referencing our internal compliance standards, including KCI-SEC-001 and KCI-SEC-002. This training is mandatory for all employees and is designed to ensure that everyone understands their role in maintaining the security of the Kestrel Cloud platform. Regular assessments are conducted to evaluate knowledge retention and application in day-to-day operations."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What mechanisms are in place to monitor and log traffic at the perimeter of your network to detect unauthorized access attempts?\nA: Kestrel Cloud integrates perimeter security measures with our compliance program, Kestrel Secure. We utilize automated logging mechanisms that capture all incoming and outgoing traffic for analysis. Our monitoring tools, aligned with ISO 27001 standards, provide real-time alerts for unauthorized access attempts. Customers are required to maintain their own security configurations while we oversee the infrastructure's integrity. Logs are reviewed bi-weekly to ensure compliance and security."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What strategies do you employ to manage and secure wireless networks within your organization?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs a rigorous compliance program that adheres to industry standards such as ISO 27001 and NIST SP 800-53. Our wireless networks are secured using enterprise-grade encryption and monitored continuously for unauthorized access. We conduct biannual audits to ensure compliance with our internal policies. Customers are responsible for managing their own access control policies within Kestrel Cloud, while we maintain the underlying infrastructure security."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How do you ensure that egress controls are effectively implemented to prevent unauthorized data exfiltration from your network?\nA: Kestrel Cloud enforces egress controls through our compliance program, which adheres to ISO 27001 standards. We utilize automated tools to monitor and log all outbound traffic, ensuring that any unauthorized attempts are flagged immediately. Our customers are responsible for configuring their own security groups, but we provide guidelines to streamline this process. Regular internal audits verify the effectiveness of our controls, maintaining a secure environment for all users of Kestrel Cloud."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What role do automated tools play in managing and enforcing egress controls across your network?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs automated tools to enforce egress controls as part of our compliance program, Kestrel Guard. These tools continuously monitor data egress points and ensure adherence to our established security standards. We maintain strict control over egress traffic, and while our tools provide significant oversight, customers must actively participate in defining their specific egress policies to align with their compliance requirements."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How frequently are the perimeter security controls, including firewalls and intrusion detection systems, tested for effectiveness and compliance with security policies?\nA: Kestrel Cloud performs effectiveness testing of perimeter security controls monthly as part of our compliance program, Kestrel Secure. Our firewalls and intrusion detection systems are evaluated against established standards, including ISO 27001 and NIST SP 800-53. This ensures that our infrastructure remains resilient and compliant. Customers are responsible for their own application security within the managed-container environment."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How frequently are your egress filtering rules reviewed and updated to adapt to new threats or changes in your network architecture?\nA: Kestrel Cloud updates its egress filtering rules biannually, in line with our compliance program, Kestrel Secure. This process is governed by our internal security standards, which are aligned with industry best practices. Customers are responsible for their own configurations, while we ensure our core infrastructure is fortified against evolving threats. Regular audits support our commitment to maintaining a secure environment."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How often are your DDoS protection measures reviewed and updated to address emerging threats and vulnerabilities?\nA: Kestrel Cloud reviews its DDoS protection measures quarterly as part of our compliance program. We adhere to the ISO 27001 standard and leverage our internal security assessments to ensure that our infrastructure remains resilient against evolving threats. Clients are responsible for their application-level security, while we maintain the integrity of our platform."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How frequently is the physical access control system reviewed and updated to ensure its effectiveness against unauthorized access?\nA: Kestrel Cloud performs a review of physical access controls quarterly, aligning with our compliance program standards. We reference the ISO 27001 framework to guide our assessments and ensure all controls remain effective against unauthorized access. The responsibility for maintaining physical security is shared with our customers, who are encouraged to implement their own access measures alongside our infrastructure."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: In what ways does your organization ensure that access controls are integrated with other security measures in place?\nA: Kestrel Cloud integrates access controls with security measures by adhering to our compliance program, specifically the Kestrel Security Framework. Access controls are enforced through automated tools, with quarterly reviews conducted to ensure alignment with our standards. Our customers are responsible for managing their own user permissions within the defined parameters of our platform. This structured approach ensures that security and access controls are maintained in tandem, enhancing overall system integrity."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: Can you explain the process for reviewing and approving changes to firewall configurations, including who is involved?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs a strict change management process for firewall configurations. Changes are reviewed bi-weekly by our security team and must align with our compliance program, Kestrel Secure. Approval requires sign-off from both the security officer and the infrastructure manager. This ensures that all changes are documented and controlled according to our internal standards."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What protocols and technologies are employed for secure remote access to your network, and how do you ensure they are regularly updated?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs TLS for secure remote access and adheres to our Compliance Framework, which includes regular audits. We ensure that all access protocols are reviewed and updated annually, in line with our internal security policies. Clients are responsible for managing their own access controls, while we maintain the infrastructure and compliance standards necessary for secure operations."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What training do employees receive regarding the physical security measures in place at your data centers, including emergency response protocols?\nA: Kestrel Cloud mandates annual training sessions for all employees on physical security protocols and emergency response measures. This training aligns with our compliance program, Kestrel Secure, ensuring that staff are well-versed in our established controls. Employees are required to complete this training to maintain their roles, with periodic assessments to reinforce knowledge retention. Customer responsibilities are clearly outlined in our service agreements, ensuring clarity in shared security obligations."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: What criteria do you use to evaluate and select vendors for physical security services related to your data centers?\nA: Kestrel Cloud employs a rigorous compliance framework to select physical security vendors. We reference our internal compliance program, Kestrel Secure, which mandates adherence to NIST SP 800-53 controls. Vendors are evaluated based on their ability to meet these standards, including access control measures and incident response capabilities. The selection process is succinct, focusing on compliance documentation and past performance metrics to ensure alignment with our security objectives."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: In what instances do you conduct dynamic application security testing (DAST), and how do you integrate the findings into your development cycle?\nA: Kestrel Cloud conducts dynamic application security testing (DAST) biannually, aligning with our compliance program standards. Findings are documented and categorized by severity, and we enforce remediation protocols as per our internal security framework. Our customers are responsible for their own application configurations, while we ensure our infrastructure remains secure. We reference ISO 27001 compliance to guide our testing practices and reporting."}
{"reference_id": "", "content": "Q: How is the effectiveness of your firewall rules assessed, and what criteria are used to determine necessary adjustments?\nA: Kestrel Cloud evaluates firewall effectiveness through biannual compliance checks aligned with our Kestrel Compliance Program. Adjustments are made based on the NIST Cybersecurity Framework and specific threat intelligence reports. The responsibility for maintaining firewall rules is shared with customers, who must ensure their configurations meet our guidelines."}
