{"entity": "Tanager Geospatial", "type": "organization", "description": "Tanager Geospatial is an organization that provides actionable insights to its customers through its hosted geospatial mapping and analytics platform, Tanager Atlas. A central tenet of its operations is a comprehensive shared-responsibility model, which meticulously defines security, compliance, and data management responsibilities between Tanager Geospatial and its customers or third-party vendors. This model is applied across various operational areas, including data protection, egress controls, wireless network management and security, and the management of third-party dependencies. The organization evaluates the security practices of its subprocessors through a comprehensive assessment process aligned with this model, conducting quarterly reviews of subprocessor compliance and bi-annual audits.\n\nTanager Geospatial prioritizes safeguarding customer data, reinforcing its shared-responsibility model during onboarding, which includes acceptable use of company resources and accountability. Security protocol violations are addressed through a structured disciplinary process, and employee understanding of acceptable use policies is maintained through quarterly training. Policy changes are communicated via quarterly updates and immediate notifications for critical changes, with a rigorous policy review process conducted every six months. Management plays a critical role in the policy lifecycle, ensuring adherence to this model.\n\nFor security, Tanager Geospatial maintains logs for 12 months using centralized logging tools and Network Time Protocol (NTP). Access control is multi-layered, incorporating robust multi-factor authentication (MFA) based on user roles and data sensitivity. Quarterly access reviews, automated tools, and an engineering team manage access, with an Access Control Board (ACB) approving changes. The shared-responsibility model covers user onboarding, offboarding, role change requests, and swift access revocation, supplemented by quarterly refresher courses. Application security integrates secure design principles throughout the development lifecycle of Tanager Atlas, with bi-weekly static application security testing (SAST) and dynamic application security testing (DAST) before major releases and quarterly during security sprints. Perimeter security controls, including firewalls and intrusion detection systems, undergo rigorous quarterly testing, and a multi-layered approach to network segmentation mitigates lateral movement risks. Firewall rules and egress filtering rules are regularly reviewed quarterly, both adhering to the shared-responsibility model. The organization also conducts quarterly vulnerability scans across all environments, utilizing industry-standard tools such as Nessus and Qualys, and adheres to a shared-responsibility model for vulnerability management. As part of its commitment to security within the shared-responsibility model, Tanager Geospatial conducts bi-annual penetration testing, engaging third-party security firms for these assessments. The organization also adheres to a strict vulnerability remediation policy and integrates automation into its vulnerability management process, especially during scanning and remediation phases. Tanager Geospatial integrates findings from vulnerability scans into its risk management framework and measures the effectiveness of its vulnerability management program.\n\nTanager Geospatial maintains a rigorous audit and compliance framework, conducting semi-annual internal audits and biannual audits of Tanager Atlas. The scope and frequency of external audits are determined by industry best practices and the shared-responsibility model. External audit findings are reviewed, tracked, prioritized, and communicated timely, with senior management and the board involved in approval. Change management follows a structured process aligned with its shared-responsibility model, assessing change requests for their impact on the Tanager Atlas platform and users. Quarterly audits of logging and monitoring practices are conducted, and the Compliance Committee is responsible for approving changes to logging and monitoring policies.\n\nFor encryption, Tanager Geospatial utilizes TLS 1.2 and above for data in transit and employs encryption protocols. Key management is a critical component of its shared-responsibility model, involving a key escrow system, secure key generation guidelines, and rotation of encryption keys every 90 days. A robust auditing framework monitors key usage within Tanager Atlas, and a stringent key destruction policy is in place. The organization provides tools and documentation for customers to manage their own encryption keys, trains personnel in cryptographic practices, and undergoes annual third-party audits.\n\nIn data management, Tanager Geospatial assesses residency requirements for different data types and manages infrastructure and compliance within its shared-responsibility model. The Chief Data Officer and compliance team approve the data classification framework. The organization adheres to the shared-responsibility model for data retention, secure data disposal, and assessing/mitigating related risks. Data minimization practices are evaluated using automated tools, and bi-annual audits ensure compliance with data residency requirements. Tanager Geospatial also adheres to a shared-responsibility model for the secure destruction of media containing sensitive data. The organization conducts backup and restore testing quarterly to ensure data integrity and availability within Tanager Atlas.\n\nTanager Geospatial's security governance program is underpinned by the NIST Cybersecurity Framework and ISO 27001 standards, with quarterly risk assessments. The organization defines ownership of its security program and clarifies roles and responsibilities for security governance through its shared-responsibility model. Risk identification and assessment are rigorous, and security risks and compliance status are reported to the board quarterly. The Compliance and Risk Management Team approves changes to the control framework, and the governance program is reviewed every six months. The organization also employs a shared-responsibility model with third-party vendors, requiring adherence to its security governance policies and conducting annual compliance audits.\n\nIn incident response, Tanager Geospatial implements a structured process, employing intrusion detection systems (IDS), security information and event management (SIEM) tools, and automated threat intelligence feeds for real-time detection. Incidents are prioritized based on potential impact on customers and service criticality within Tanager Atlas. Incident response analysts investigate security incidents using digital forensics and incident response methodologies. A structured approach is used to preserve forensic evidence, involving both the internal team and customers under the shared-responsibility model. Post-incident reviews are conducted, with lessons learned tracked and documented. A structured communication protocol is employed during incident responses, assessing incidents based on severity, potential impact on customer data, and compliance obligations, with a protocol for notifying affected customers. Tanager Geospatial has incident response procedures for handling detected anomalies in egress traffic, structured around a shared-responsibility model.\n\nFinally, Tanager Geospatial implements a comprehensive Mobile Device Management (MDM) solution, has a structured patch management process, and initiates a multi-step response to secure data in the event of a lost corporate device. The organization employs a multi-layered approach to monitor and log traffic at the perimeter of its network and integrates solutions from leading vendors into its security architecture for DDoS protection. It also employs a multi-layered approach to monitor and control outbound traffic, secures infrastructure, and provides customers with detailed reports. Tanager Geospatial implements a thorough background screening process for all personnel prior to onboarding and updates its personnel security training program every six months. The organization implements a robust offboarding procedure to ensure the timely revocation of access for departing employees. Tanager Geospatial implements a robust shared-responsibility model for physical security at its data centers, conducting comprehensive reviews of its physical access control systems every six months, employing a rigorous visitor management protocol, and implementing CCTV coverage and environmental monitoring systems. The organization conducts Business Impact Analysis (BIA) through a structured process and conducts quarterly failover testing to ensure the resilience of Tanager Atlas. It integrates pandemic response planning into its business continuity strategy through an annual comprehensive risk assessment process and implements a rigorous training program for its employees on business continuity procedures, aligned with a shared-responsibility model. Tanager Geospatial conducts full-scale disaster recovery exercises biannually and assesses the effectiveness of its communication strategies during disaster recovery events, using a shared-responsibility model for documenting lessons learned. The organization maintains awareness of emerging threats through continuous monitoring, threat intelligence feeds, and regular risk assessments, and conducts comprehensive semi-annual reviews of its vendor management policies. Tanager Geospatial also maintains comprehensive documentation to demonstrate compliance with its vendor management policies and evaluates the effectiveness of its exit strategies. The organization employs a rigorous vulnerability management process, which is overseen by its Chief Security Officer. This process adheres to industry standards such as ISO 27001 and NIST SP 800-53, and includes quarterly vulnerability assessments."}
